/**
 * A persisted record of one route request and what the model answered.
 *
 * Serves three purposes, in increasing order of how much they matter on demo day:
 *
 * 1. **Prompt tuning.** {@link RouteQuery#routeLabels} next to
 *    {@link RouteQuery#validationError} and {@link RouteQuery#retried} shows
 *    exactly which trips the model gets wrong and how, which is the only reliable
 *    way to decide what to add to {@link BuildingFacts#facts}.
 * 2. **Cost and latency accounting.** {@link RouteQuery#cachedTokens} against
 *    {@link RouteQuery#promptTokens} proves the prompt cache is actually being
 *    hit — a cache that silently stopped working looks identical to one that
 *    works, apart from the bill.
 * 3. **An offline fallback.** A row with {@link RouteQuery#isPinned} true is a
 *    known-good answer for a verified demo route, replayed by
 *    {@link RoomFinder#findRoute} when the model is unavailable. This is the
 *    mitigation for an API outage or rate limit during the pitch.
 *
 * Every request is logged, successes and failures alike, because a failed request
 * is the more interesting record. Writing the row never blocks the response: a
 * logging failure must not turn a good route into an error.
 */
entity type RouteQuery {

  /**
   * The building the request was made against.
   */
  building: !Building

  /**
   * The user's message, exactly as typed, e.g.
   * "I'm on floor 2 near 2240, need to get to 1641".
   *
   * Kept verbatim for prompt tuning — the phrasing *is* the test case.
   */
  userMessage: !string

  /**
   * Lower-cased, whitespace-collapsed form of {@link RouteQuery#userMessage},
   * used as the lookup key for the fallback cache.
   *
   * Normalizing means a demo chip tapped twice, or re-typed with different
   * spacing or casing, still finds its pinned answer. It is intentionally a crude
   * exact-match key rather than a semantic one: a fallback that silently answers a
   * *similar* question with the wrong route is worse at a demo than no fallback.
   */
  normalizedMessage: !string

  /**
   * Outcome of the request: `ok` when a route came back, `clarify` when the model
   * asked a question instead, `error` when no usable answer could be produced.
   *
   * Mirrors {@link VisionRoute#status}; see that field for the full contract.
   */
  status: !string

  /**
   * The ordered waypoint labels the model returned, after normalization and after
   * validation passed, e.g. `["2240","2-W14","S1","1-W3","1641"]`.
   *
   * Empty for a `clarify` or `error` outcome. These are labels rather than
   * {@link Waypoint} ids so that a pinned row stays replayable after waypoints are
   * re-generated and their ids change.
   */
  routeLabels: [string]

  /**
   * The human-readable instructions the model wrote, one per leg.
   */
  steps: [string]

  /**
   * The model's question when {@link RouteQuery#status} is `clarify` — e.g. which
   * of two similarly numbered rooms was meant. Null otherwise.
   */
  question: string

  /**
   * Whether every returned label resolved to a {@link Waypoint}, so the frontend
   * could draw the line.
   *
   * False with {@link RouteQuery#status} `ok` is the specific degraded case worth
   * watching: the model produced sensible instructions but at least one label
   * could not be placed, so the user got text steps and no drawn route.
   */
  drawable: boolean

  /**
   * Whether the one permitted retry was used — i.e. the first answer failed
   * validation and was sent back to the model with the error.
   *
   * A rising rate here is the earliest signal that the labels on the plans have
   * become hard to read, or that {@link BuildingFacts#facts} is missing something.
   */
  retried: boolean

  /**
   * Why validation rejected the model's answer, e.g. an unknown label or an
   * illegal floor change. Null when validation passed.
   *
   * Populated even when the retry subsequently succeeded, so the failure mode is
   * not lost.
   */
  validationError: string

  /**
   * Wall-clock duration of the whole request in milliseconds, including any retry.
   *
   * The number the loading-state copy has to cover; typically a few seconds.
   */
  latencyMs: int

  /**
   * Identifier of the model that answered, e.g. the configured completion client's
   * model name.
   *
   * Recorded because accuracy on these routes is strongly model-dependent, so a
   * logged route is only meaningful next to the model that produced it.
   */
  modelName: string

  /**
   * Total input tokens billed for the request, images included.
   */
  promptTokens: int

  /**
   * How many of {@link RouteQuery#promptTokens} were served from the prompt cache.
   *
   * Should be close to the full prompt size on every request after the first,
   * because the cached prefix — the facts and all the floor images — is identical
   * each time. A sustained zero here means caching has broken and every request is
   * paying full price for the same images.
   */
  cachedTokens: int

  /**
   * Output tokens generated. Dominates latency, since the input is cached.
   */
  completionTokens: int

  /**
   * Whether this row is a curated fallback answer.
   *
   * Pinned rows are the verified demo routes, and are the only rows
   * {@link RoomFinder#findRoute} will replay when the model cannot be reached.
   * Pinning is a manual, deliberate act: it asserts "a human has walked this route
   * and this answer is correct", which no automatic heuristic can establish.
   */
  isPinned: boolean
}
